WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.810
The old man's hour had come!

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:08.430
With a loud shout, I threw open the lantern and burst into the room.

3
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.630
He cried once — once only.

4
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.990
Without delay, I forced him to the floor, and pulled the heavy bed over him.

5
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:23.770
I then smiled, to find the action so far done.

6
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:31.790
But, for many minutes, the heart beat on with a quiet sound.

7
00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:37.930
This, however, did not concern me; it would not be heard through the wall.

8
00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.290
At length, it stopped.

9
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.770
The old man was dead.

10
00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.850
I removed the bed and examined the body.

11
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:55.690
I placed my hand over his heart and held it there many minutes.

12
00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.310
There was no movement.

13
00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.210
He was stone dead.

14
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.870
His eye would trouble me no more.

15
00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:22.350
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise steps I took for hiding the body.

16
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.810
I worked quickly, but in silence.

17
00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.050
First of all, I took apart the body.

18
00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:33.450
I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

19
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:40.830
I then took up three pieces of wood from the flooring, and placed his body parts under the room.

20
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:52.650
I then replaced the wooden boards so well that no human eye — not even his — could have seen anything wrong.

21
00:01:54.140 --> 00:02:00.030
There was nothing to wash out — no mark of any kind — no blood whatever.

22
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.210
I had been too smart for that.

23
00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.010
A tub had caught all — ha! ha!

24
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.210
When I had made an end of these labors, it was four o'clock in the morning.

25
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.890
As a clock sounded the hour, there came a noise at the street door.

26
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.330
I went down to open it with a light heart — for what had I now to fear?

27
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:25.590
There entered three men, who said they were officers of the police.

28
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:39.890
A cry had been heard by a neighbor during the night; suspicion of a crime had been aroused; information had been given at the police office, and the officers had been sent to search the building.

29
00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:45.450
I smiled — for what had I to fear?

30
00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.970
The cry, I said, was my own in a dream.

31
00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.270
The old man, I said, was not in the country.

32
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:58.150
I took my visitors all over the house.

33
00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.730
I told them to search — search well.

34
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.530
I led them, at length, to his room.

35
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.090
I brought chairs there, and told them to rest.

36
00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:15.270
I placed my own seat upon the very place under which lay the body of the victim.

37
00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.230
The officers were satisfied.

38
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.330
I was completely at ease.

39
00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.810
They sat, and while I answered happily, they talked of common things.

40
00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:34.270
But, after a while, I felt myself getting weak and wished them gone.

41
00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.770
My head hurt, and I had a ringing in my ears; but still they sat and talked.

42
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.050
The ringing became more severe.

43
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.490
I talked more freely to do away with the feeling.

44
00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:50.210
But it continued until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

45
00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.270
I talked more and with a heightened voice.

46
00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.710
Yet the sound increased — and what could I do?

47
00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:02.490
It was a low, dull, quick sound like a watch makes when inside a piece of cotton.

48
00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:06.350
I had trouble breathing — and yet the officers heard it not.

49
00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:10.230
I talked more quickly — more loudly; but the noise increased.

50
00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:16.210
I stood up and argued about silly things, in a high voice and with violent hand movements.

51
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.610
But the noise kept increasing.

52
00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:21.490
Why would they not be gone?

53
00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:31.090
I walked across the floor with heavy steps, as if excited to anger by the observations of the men — but the noise increased.

54
00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.750
What could I do?

55
00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:39.750
I swung my chair and moved it upon the floor, but the noise continually increased.

56
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:43.530
It grew louder — louder — louder!

57
00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:48.610
And still the men talked pleasantly, and smiled.

58
00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.390
Was it possible they heard not?

59
00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:54.230
No, no! They heard!

60
00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:55.230
They suspected!

61
00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:56.390
They knew!

62
00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.750
They were making a joke of my horror!

63
00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:02.050
This I thought, and this I think.

64
00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.570
But anything was better than this pain!

65
00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.870
I could bear those smiles no longer!

66
00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:11.930
I felt that I must scream or die!

67
00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.830
And now — again! Louder! Louder! Louder!

68
00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.770
"Villains!" I cried, "Pretend no more!

69
00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.390
I admit the deed!

70
00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.050
Tear up the floor boards! Here, here!

71
00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.090
It is the beating of his hideous heart!"
